- **Breaking change:** `fleetctl gitops` now resets every setting omitted from `org_settings` and fleet `settings` to its default, including `gitops`, `host_expiry_settings`, and `smtp_settings`. If your YAML doesn't include `smtp_settings`, GitOps clears the SMTP settings and password stored in Fleet. Fleet then stops sending invite, password reset, and multi-factor authentication (MFA) emails, so users who log in with MFA can't log in. If your YAML doesn't include `gitops`, GitOps mode turns off and the repository URL is removed, so settings can be edited in the Fleet UI again. Add these settings to your YAML before you run GitOps with Fleet 5. `fleetctl gitops` warns before resetting `smtp_settings`, `gitops`, or `server_settings` values. Not reset: `server_settings.server_url`, `vulnerability_settings.databases_path`, and `labels`, `software`, and `secrets` when their GitOps mode exception is on (for example, omitted enroll secrets stay valid).
- **Breaking change:** `PATCH /api/v1/fleet/config` and `fleetctl gitops` now require `smtp_settings.password` when changing the SMTP `server`, `port`, `user_name`, or `authentication_type`, so the stored password is never sent to a different SMTP server. A blank `smtp_settings.password` keeps the password stored in Fleet when those settings don't change.
- Added `gitops` and `smtp_settings` to `fleetctl generate-gitops` output.
- Changed `GET /api/v1/fleet/config` to return `smtp_settings` (with the password masked) to users with the global GitOps role.
